6. The learned counsel for the petitioner has relied upon the decisions reported in;
1. 2018 ACJ 2465, High Court of Punjab and Haryana, Palo Vs Rajesh Sahni and another.
2. 2018 ACJ 2656, High Court of Bombay, Chandrakanth Vs. Majlasbai and other.

However he himself has relied upon another decision which is reported in 2019 ACJ 1931 Hon'ble High Court of Karnataka, Bengaluru wherein it is held that;
Negligence - contributory negligence - minor - driving license - absence of - lorry driven at fast speed dashed against a motorcycle resulting in death of motorcyclist - tribunal found that since deceased was a minor who was not holding a driving license he contributed to the accident - tribunal held that lorry driver was negligent to the extent of 75% and deceased contributed to the accident to the extent of 25%  tribuna's finding upheld.
Similarly in the case on hand also, petitioner was minor at the time of the accident and not holding driving license and in the junction the accident had occurred due to minor negligence on the part of the petitioner and as well as major negligence on the part of the driver of offending car.

Thus, I am of the considered opinion that, the SCCH14 23 MVC No.726/2019 petitioner is entitled for the compensation under the following heads;
1. Pain and sufferings Rs. 30,000/
2. Nourishment, conveyance and Rs. 5,000/ attendant charges 3.

